!type: Basic Math
!deck: Math::Calculus::Differentiation
!tags: Math Calculus Differentiation Theory

!id: zzKitpg6bx
!front: What is a critical point?
!back: A critical point of \( f \) is an interior point \( c \) of the domain of \( f \) at which either \( f'(c) = 0 \) or \( f'(c) \) fails to exist.
~~~

!id: GNUskJIVPy
!front: What is the main idea of the first derivative test?
!back: The first derivative test classifies a critical point \( c \) as a local maximum, a local minimum, or inconclusive by comparing the sign of \( f' \) on the two halves of a punctured neighborhood of \( c \).
~~~

!id: cLa5APUShK
!front: Give a physical interpretation of the first derivative test when \( f \) is position.
!back: Then \( f' \) is signed velocity. If \( f' \) changes from positive to negative at \( c \), the motion changes from moving upward to moving downward, so \( f(c) \) is a local maximum. If \( f' \) changes from negative to positive, the motion changes from moving downward to moving upward, so \( f(c) \) is a local minimum.
~~~

!id: ZBp09xCVc5
!front: What hypotheses are needed to use the first derivative test at a critical point \( c \)?
!back: \( f \) must be continuous at \( c \) and differentiable on a punctured neighborhood of \( c \); \( f'(c) \) itself may be zero or fail to exist.
~~~

!id: 6AllLxbkmN
!front: In the first derivative test, what does \( f' \) changing sign from positive to negative across \( c \) imply?
!back: \( f \) has a strict local maximum at \( c \).
~~~

!id: eOBg5g4d3w
!front: In the first derivative test, what does \( f' \) changing sign from negative to positive across \( c \) imply?
!back: \( f \) has a strict local minimum at \( c \).
~~~

!id: hivA9FTtp5
!front: In the first derivative test, what does it mean if \( f' \) is positive throughout a punctured neighborhood of \( c \)?
!back: \( f \) is strictly increasing through \( c \), so \( c \) is not a local extremum.
~~~

!id: TqaJZVuBH3
!front: In the first derivative test, what does it mean if \( f' \) is negative throughout a punctured neighborhood of \( c \)?
!back: \( f \) is strictly decreasing through \( c \), so \( c \) is not a local extremum.
~~~

!id: Kl0wfuwlv0
!front: Why does \( f' \) changing sign from positive to negative indicate a local maximum?
!back: \( f' > 0 \) immediately to the left of \( c \) means \( f \) is increasing up to \( c \); \( f' < 0 \) immediately to the right means \( f \) is decreasing away from \( c \). Hence \( f(x) < f(c) \) for every \( x \) in a punctured neighborhood of \( c \).
~~~

!id: 0oiaZNkT0Y
!front: Why does \( f' \) changing sign from negative to positive indicate a local minimum?
!back: \( f' < 0 \) immediately to the left of \( c \) means \( f \) is decreasing down to \( c \); \( f' > 0 \) immediately to the right means \( f \) is increasing away from \( c \). Hence \( f(x) > f(c) \) for every \( x \) in a punctured neighborhood of \( c \).
~~~

!id: 5PCIVmIycT
!front: Give an example of a stationary critical point that is not a local extremum.
!back: For \( f(x) = x^3 \), \( f'(0) = 0 \), but \( f'(x) = 3x^2 > 0 \) for every \( x \ne 0 \). So \( f \) is strictly increasing through \( 0 \), and \( 0 \) is not a local extremum.
~~~

!id: iZBE6xycSb
!front: What is the main idea of the second derivative test?
!back: The second derivative test classifies a stationary critical point \( c \) (where \( f'(c) = 0 \)) as a local maximum, a local minimum, or inconclusive, using the sign of \( f''(c) \).
~~~

!id: WoprBtQYRq
!front: Give a physical interpretation of the second derivative test when \( f \) is position.
!back: Then \( f'' \) is acceleration. At a stationary point \( c \), where \( f'(c) = 0 \), positive acceleration means the graph bends upward near the flat tangent, giving a local minimum; negative acceleration means it bends downward, giving a local maximum.
~~~

!id: hQVhdbKFL8
!front: What hypotheses are needed to use the second derivative test at \( c \)?
!back: \( f'(c) = 0 \), and \( f''(c) \) exists as a finite second-order derivative at the single point \( c \).
~~~

!id: KuycefiBQ8
!front: In the second derivative test, what does \( f''(c) > 0 \) imply when \( f'(c) = 0 \)?
!back: \( f \) has a strict local minimum at \( c \).
~~~

!id: lK3nBoG7DS
!front: In the second derivative test, what does \( f''(c) < 0 \) imply when \( f'(c) = 0 \)?
!back: \( f \) has a strict local maximum at \( c \).
~~~

!id: gndbllwJqo
!front: In the second derivative test, what does \( f''(c) = 0 \) imply?
!back: The test is inconclusive: \( c \) may be a local minimum, a local maximum, or neither.
~~~

!id: v6Xvb6755z
!front: If \( f'' \) is continuous and \( f''(c) > 0 \), what does this imply about \( f' \) near \( c \)?
!back: Continuity of \( f'' \) means \( f'' > 0 \) not just at \( c \) but throughout some neighborhood of \( c \). Since \( f'' \) is the derivative of \( f' \), this makes \( f' \) strictly increasing on that neighborhood.
~~~

!id: KKNFd5iBoW
!front: If \( f'' \) is continuous and \( f''(c) < 0 \), what does this imply about \( f' \) near \( c \)?
!back: Continuity of \( f'' \) means \( f'' < 0 \) not just at \( c \) but throughout some neighborhood of \( c \). Since \( f'' \) is the derivative of \( f' \), this makes \( f' \) strictly decreasing on that neighborhood.
~~~

!id: pCV9LoXhmk
!front: Why does \( f''(c) > 0 \), together with \( f'(c) = 0 \), indicate a local minimum?
!back: \( f''(c) > 0 \) makes \( f' \) strictly increasing through \( c \). Combined with \( f'(c) = 0 \), \( f' \) is negative just before \( c \) and positive just after, so the first derivative test gives a strict local minimum.
~~~

!id: bQYfeBqPRF
!front: Why does \( f''(c) < 0 \), together with \( f'(c) = 0 \), indicate a local maximum?
!back: \( f''(c) < 0 \) makes \( f' \) strictly decreasing through \( c \). Combined with \( f'(c) = 0 \), \( f' \) is positive just before \( c \) and negative just after, so the first derivative test gives a strict local maximum.
~~~

!id: PE69rMlBNy
!front: Give an example where \( f''(c) = 0 \) but \( c \) is a local minimum.
!back: \( f(x) = x^4 \) at \( c = 0 \): \( f'(0) = 0 \), \( f''(0) = 0 \), and \( f(x) \ge f(0) \) on a neighborhood of \( 0 \).
~~~

!id: 5KjIeNMbeS
!front: Give an example where \( f''(c) = 0 \) but \( c \) is a local maximum.
!back: \( f(x) = -x^4 \) at \( c = 0 \): \( f'(0) = 0 \), \( f''(0) = 0 \), and \( f(x) \le f(0) \) on a neighborhood of \( 0 \).
~~~

!id: Iw4CpH3lwc
!front: Give an example where \( f''(c) = 0 \) and \( c \) is not a local extremum.
!back: \( f(x) = x^3 \) at \( c = 0 \): \( f'(0) = 0 \), \( f''(0) = 0 \), but \( f \) is strictly increasing through \( 0 \).
~~~

!id: kYHL8gErG0
!front: Why can the first derivative test classify some points where the second derivative test cannot?
!back: The first derivative test uses the sign of \( f' \) on a punctured neighborhood of \( c \), while the second derivative test uses only the single value \( f''(c) \). When \( f''(c) = 0 \) or fails to exist, the second derivative test is inconclusive, but the first derivative test may still resolve the sign behavior of \( f' \).
~~~

!id: hpI2gfu2jd
!front: Why do derivative tests classify local extrema rather than absolute extrema by themselves?
!back: They describe behavior only on a neighborhood of a critical point. Absolute extrema require comparing values across the entire domain or interval, including endpoints when relevant.
~~~

!id: 0dc9nxVRUY
!front: Give a geometric interpretation of a function being concave up on an interval.
!back: On that interval, the graph of \( f \) lies above (or on) every one of its tangent lines.
~~~

!id: TwPfydUnYy
!front: Give a geometric interpretation of a function being concave down on an interval.
!back: On that interval, the graph of \( f \) lies below (or on) every one of its tangent lines.
~~~

!id: BdQFccaRgN
!front: How is concavity of \( f \) on an interval \( I \) related to the monotonicity of \( f' \)?
!back: \( f \) is concave up on an interval \( I \) exactly when \( f' \) is strictly increasing on \( I \), and concave down exactly when \( f' \) is strictly decreasing on \( I \).
~~~

!id: 1PWJif61Og
!front: For \( f \) twice differentiable on an interval \( I \), what does \( f''(x) > 0 \) on \( I \) imply about concavity?
!back: \( f \) is concave up on \( I \), since \( f'' > 0 \) on \( I \) means \( f' \) is strictly increasing on \( I \) (by the Mean Value Theorem applied to \( f' \)).
~~~

!id: Z31QV1NBIZ
!front: For \( f \) twice differentiable on an interval \( I \), what does \( f''(x) < 0 \) on \( I \) imply about concavity?
!back: \( f \) is concave down on \( I \), since \( f'' < 0 \) on \( I \) means \( f' \) is strictly decreasing on \( I \) (by the Mean Value Theorem applied to \( f' \)).
~~~

!id: Ef6m6NkEhu
!front: What is an inflection point of \( f \)?
!back: A point \( c \) at which \( f \) is continuous and the concavity of \( f \) reverses as \( x \) crosses \( c \): on a punctured neighborhood of \( c \), \( f \) is concave up on one side and concave down on the other.
~~~

!id: 76QRIe0yYc
!front: If \( f''(c) \) exists and \( c \) is an inflection point, what must be true of \( f''(c) \), and why?
!back: \( f''(c) = 0 \). Since concavity reverses at \( c \), \( f' \) has a local extremum at \( c \).
!extra: Necessary, not sufficient — \( f''(c) = 0 \) alone does not guarantee \( c \) is an inflection point.
~~~

!id: 3v9WbaSYKt
!front: Give a counterexample showing \( f''(c) = 0 \) does not imply \( c \) is an inflection point.
!back:
\[ f(x) = x^4, \qquad f''(x) = 12x^2. \]
Then \( f''(0) = 0 \), but \( f''(x) \ge 0 \) everywhere, so \( f \) is concave up on all of \( \mathbb{R} \) and the concavity never reverses at \( 0 \).
!related: The same function already shows \( f''(c) = 0 \) does not determine whether \( c \) is a local extremum.
~~~

!id: 93PmhfKWxk
!front: Verify that \( f(x) = x^3 \) has an inflection point at \( x = 0 \).
!back:
\[ f''(x) = 6x, \]
negative for \( x < 0 \) and positive for \( x > 0 \). So \( f \) is concave down on \( (-\infty, 0) \) and concave up on \( (0, \infty) \); concavity reverses at \( 0 \), so \( 0 \) is an inflection point.
~~~

!id: XCKBd5w3hb
!front: How does concavity at a critical point relate to the second derivative test?
!back:
If \( f'(c) = 0 \) and \( f''(c) > 0 \) (\( f \) is concave up on a neighborhood of \( c \)), then \( c \) is a local minimum.

If \( f'(c) = 0 \) and \( f''(c) < 0 \) (\( f \) is concave down on a neighborhood of \( c \)), then \( c \) is a local maximum.
~~~
